<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lesya Ukrainka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1871">1871</a>–<a href="/years/1913">1913</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Larysa Petrivna Kosach, Lesia Ukrainka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lesya Ukrainka, born Larysa Petrivna Kosach in Novohrad-Volynskyi in 1871, was one of the foremost writers of Ukrainian literature, best known for her poems and plays, and an activist for political, civil and women's rights. The daughter of the writer Olena Pchilka and niece of Mykhailo Drahomanov, she grew up in a household where only Ukrainian was spoken and wrote her first poem at eight. Her works include the collections On the Wings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> (1893) and Thoughts and Dreams (1899) and the plays Cassandra, In the Catacombs (1905) and Forest Song (1911). Ill with tuberculosis from childhood, she died on 1 August 1913.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lesya-ukrainka.jpg" alt="Lesya Ukrainka" width="330" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Krzanowski · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lesya_Ukrainka_1901_(facecrop).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lesya Ukrainka was the second child of Petro Kosach, a lawyer who financially supported Ukrainian publishing, and of Olha Drahomanova-Kosach, who wrote as Olena Pchilka. Her uncle Mykhailo Drahomanov, a historian and folklorist, was her spiritual mentor. To avoid schools that taught in Russian, the children were educated at home by Ukrainian tutors. She knew English, German, French, Italian, Greek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, Polish, Russian and Bulgarian.</p><p>Her first published poems, &quot;Lily of the Valley&quot; and &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sappho">Sappho</a>&quot;, appeared in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a> magazine Zorya in 1884 under her pseudonym, which her mother suggested because Ukrainian publications were forbidden in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. Tuberculosis of the bone, which began in 1882 with a painful lump on her hand, kept her from school; in 1888 she received a leg <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prosthesis">prosthesis</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1888 she and her brother founded Pleyada, a literary circle that promoted Ukrainian literature and the translation of foreign classics. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taras-shevchenko">Taras Shevchenko</a> and Ivan Franko inspired her early poetry. On the Wings of Songs was printed in 1893 in Western Ukraine, then part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, and smuggled into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>. She followed it with Thoughts and Dreams in 1899 and a third collection in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernivtsi">Chernivtsi</a> in 1902.</p><p>From the mid-1890s she also wrote plays. The Blue Rose of 1896 turned Ukrainian drama from the peasantry to the intelligentsia. Her health forced her to live for long periods in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. She opposed tsarism, belonged to Ukrainian Marxist organisations and gave Mykola Hankevich a translation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto">The Communist Manifesto</a> in 1901. Police arrested her briefly in 1907.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1907 she married Klyment Kvitka. Her illness worsened to tuberculosis of the bone, lungs and kidneys, and she died in the early hours of 1 August 1913, with her mother beside her. Crowds met her coffin at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> railway station on 7 August, and despite a police ban on gatherings the funeral procession went to Baikove Cemetery.</p><p>Monuments to her stand in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> theatre and a boulevard bear her name, and the National Bank of Ukraine put her on a 200 hryvnia banknote in 2020.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lesya Ukrainka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
